      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To study the relation between Respiratory Syncytial Virus infection and asthma development by measuring airway responsiveness (AR) and M2R function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Guinea pigs (n = 34) were randomly divided into 4 groups: Hep-2/NS group (group A, n = 9), RSV/NS group (group B, n =9), Hep-2/OVA group (group C, n = 8) and RSV/OVA group(group D, n = 8). On day 21 after infection we tested AR and M2R. Then counted eosinophils in BALF and observed pathological change."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Intraairway pressure(IP mmH20) of group B had no significant difference with group A(P > 0.01), and the extent of IP decrease also had no difference between groups A and B (P > 0. 05), but IP of C group were much higher than group A (P<0.05), with extent of IP decrease lower than group A (P < 0.05). And IP of group D were higher than group C (P < 0.01), with the extent of IP decrease much lower than group C (P < 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "RSV infection could enhance OVA-induced M2R dysfunction, then develop AHR."
        }
      ]},
